Terra-Unwired-Depth: Lares (Tasked Spirit). A read-only, ZERO-TRUST follow-up to an earlier review (its verdict sits at /home/joshu/.lares-scratch/controller/terra-unwired-review/final.md — read it first). Repo: /home/joshu/Synthetic-Dream-Machine at the span head ffe05bc79. Read code and canon (bags/**/*.mem) directly; write nothing. Trust no comment, test or canon claim until the code bears it out. Mark every claim MEASURED (file:line read) or INFER.

The controller holds five defaults and six uncertainties. Settle each question with evidence; where the code cannot settle it, say UNSETTLED and name what would.

Q1 (K23 presenter binding). Defaults: `verifyDelegation` (packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-identity-slot.ts ~250) should take the AUTHENTICATED presenter from the session and refuse unless it equals `payload.audience`. Row 7's S3b landed per-face ward keys in the admission with proof of possession (PR #171). TRACE: does any landed share/sync path (node and browser) already hold an authenticated per-peer identity at the point a capability token would be checked (the sealed face gate handshake, the admission, the session)? Name the exact variable/function where a presenter identity is proven, and whether it reaches the share path. Is S3b's proof of possession the right binding, or a different one (the socket handshake's peer key)?

Q2 (deriveActorId). Default: retire it; let the Automerge repo assign per-writer actor ids. MEASURE: how does production assign Automerge actor ids today (node and browser, per tab)? Would wiring deriveActorId collide concurrent writers of one face? Is anything else per-DID actor-keyed?

Q3 (P27b secrets). Default: retire the in-memory registry; make `secrets` a REQUIRED named reading in `makeTierFloorSource` (packages/lararium-mesh/src/cap-tier.ts ~206) so every site passes NO_CONTRACT_SECRETS_PLANE explicitly. MEASURE: list every production caller of makeTierFloorSource and whether each passes `secrets`; does any production store hold secrets today (anything that should floor CONTRACT)? Would the required-reading change break any caller?

Q4 (P27a declared tier). Default: wire `capTierShareDecision` (packages/lararium-mesh/src/federation-gate.ts ~550) at every crossing and bulb read, node and browser, early returns included. MEASURE: enumerate every share/announce/access decision point on node (packages/lararium-node/src/self-slot-share.ts and siblings) and browser (packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts ~2095 and siblings), each early return, and whether a declared-tier datum source (DeclaredTierSource) exists in production to feed it. Size the wiring: how many sites.

Q5 (K22 + L28, K18). Defaults: retire `reconstructAndReadmit`/`runReadmitEdge` as K18's path (keep the Shamir primitive for self-recovery); reshape `rollEnrolments` (packages/lararium-mesh/src/persona-group-secret.ts ~227) inside a fresh K18 rotation ceremony with wipeable buffers and L24's device-edge renewal. MEASURE: what of K18's ceremony already stands landed (road row K18, PR #135: rotation, reanchorAfterRotation, recovery contacts' quorum)? What exactly is missing between the landed parts and a production recovery verb? Size it. Is any K18 piece a founding blocker in the code's own terms (does founding or join call anything that needs it)?

Q6 (the stuck joinee). Since R1, a replay after a lease roll refuses `replay-edge-lapsed` (the row7-s3a lane, PR #168). Owed ruling (a) or (b) names the way out for a seated admission whose edge lapsed across a lease roll; find the two options in canon (grep the road and bags/lares/ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/seat-harness.mem for replay-edge-lapsed / stuck joinee / L24). Does L24's renewal at the roll (L28's work) resolve the stuck joinee by itself, so the fork folds into Q5's lane? Which option does the code favor?

OUTPUT (final message, under 1100 words): per question: the answer, MEASURED/INFER cites, whether the controller's default stands, bends or breaks, and the size of the work. End with any defect you found that the earlier review missed.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/seat-law`>>

!! SEAT LAW (appended by tools/launch-codex.mjs to every brief; it binds this seat)
* A lane launches no Sol seat; the controller's one Sol read per train runs at the read floor of 0%.
* Stay inside the wall. Approval never escalates; a build seat has no network, loopback included; only a research seat takes `--search`. A step that needs more than the wall stops and names itself in `out.md`; the wall holds by refusal.
* Never commit and never run a git write. The lead commits each red, then each fix.
* Never build (`pnpm build`), never run vitest browser mode, never stand a vessel, socket or daemon. Typecheck (`tsc -p … --noEmit`) and run single test files that open no socket; mark any test that needs a socket "lead runs".
* A "stale build" from the dist guard stops the run; name it, and the lead builds.
* A read seat writes no file: its final message carries the hand-back. Any other seat writes `out.md` and every note in the seat directory alone. `-o` names `final.md`; stdin closes (`< /dev/null`); `TMPDIR` and `TMPPREFIX` sit inside the seat directory.
* Never run the battery; the lead holds its lock. Never read a credential file.
* Launch without `--ephemeral`: each seat keeps its rollout, so the spend tally counts it.
* The lead runs `pnpm build` in its worktree after every span merge and before each build seat; a verdict read off a stale dist counts as unread.

